C = [
("h1", "8. The Bride, the Lamb’s Wife: Revelation 21 and 22"),
("p", "Claim: when the angel says, “Come hither, I will shew thee the bride, the Lamb’s wife,” what he shows is a city. The city is the new Jerusalem, and its gates carry the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel. This is the pattern of Isaiah 62, where the city and the land are the wife and the sons are the ones who marry her. The city names the people who dwell in it, and the people are the redeemed of Israel. The nations are named separately, and they walk in the city’s light."),
("sub", "8.1 The city prepared as a bride"),
("p", "John first sees the city from a distance and describes it with a simile: “I John saw the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down from God out of heaven, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband” (Revelation 21:2). The “as” matters. The city is likened to a bride. The voice that follows speaks in the language of Israel’s covenant: “Behold, the tabernacle of God is with men, and he will dwell with them, and they shall be his people, and God himself shall be with them, and be their God” (Revelation 21:3). The clauses “they shall be his people” and “be their God” are the clauses of the covenant formula that Jeremiah gives to the house of Israel: “and will be their God, and they shall be my people” (Jeremiah 31:33)."),
("sub", "8.2 The angel’s showing"),
("p", "The identification is made by the angel and not by the reader. “Come hither, I will shew thee the bride, the Lamb’s wife” (Revelation 21:9). The next verse says what he showed: “and shewed me that great city, the holy Jerusalem, descending out of heaven from God” (Revelation 21:10). The bride that John is told he will see is the city that he sees. The city is described in the verses that follow, and the description includes the names that are written on it."),
("p", "“And had a wall great and high, and had twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel” (Revelation 21:12). The gates are set in order on the four sides, three on each (Revelation 21:13). The foundations carry names also: “the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb” (Revelation 21:14). The city that is called the bride is entered by gates that bear the names of the tribes of Israel and is founded on twelve names of the Lamb’s apostles. The tribes are named, and the text does not hide them."),
("sub", "8.3 The city and the people"),
("p", "A reader may ask how a city can be a bride. Isaiah has already answered. In the sixty-second chapter the land is called Beulah, which is married, and the people are the sons who marry her: “so shall thy sons marry thee” (Isaiah 62:5). The city and the land are the wife, the sons are the people, and the bridegroom rejoices over the bride. The figure moves between the city and her inhabitants because the prophets treat them as one. Revelation does the same. The Lamb’s wife is the city, and the city is the people who are named upon her gates. This is the way the figure works in the prophets, and the volume follows it."),
("p", "There is a second link. In the letter to the church in Philadelphia the Lord promises the one who overcomes, “I will write upon him the name of my God, and the name of the city of my God, which is new Jerusalem” (Revelation 3:12). The overcomer carries the name of the city. A city whose name is written on its people is a city that names its people. The promise is made within the book’s own setting, and it does not say that the Body of Christ is the overcomer. It says that the people of the city bear the city’s name."),
("sub", "8.4 The nations and the bride’s voice"),
("p", "The nations are not inside the wife. They are the ones who walk in her light: “And the nations of them which are saved shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth do bring their glory and honour into it” (Revelation 21:24). That is the picture of Isaiah 62:2, in which “the Gentiles shall see thy righteousness, and all kings thy glory.” The city is the center, and the nations come to it. The book also keeps the gate open for them: “the gates of it shall not be shut at all by day” (Revelation 21:25)."),
("p", "At the very end the bride speaks. “And the Spirit and the bride say, Come. And let him that heareth say, Come. And let him that is athirst come” (Revelation 22:17). The invitation goes out from the Spirit and the bride, and it is taken up by the hearer and the thirsty. The bride is a party who calls and not only a party who is called. She is the wife of the Lamb, and she bids the world come to the water of life."),
("tbl", "The table follows the city, the names, the nations and the bride’s voice.", [
    ("Rev 21:2", "“prepared as a bride adorned for her husband”", "The city is likened to a bride"),
    ("Rev 21:3", "“they shall be his people, and God himself shall be with them, and be their God”", "The covenant formula of the prophets"),
    ("Rev 21:9", "“Come hither, I will shew thee the bride, the Lamb’s wife”", "The angel promises to show the bride"),
    ("Rev 21:10", "“that great city, the holy Jerusalem, descending out of heaven from God”", "What he shows is the city"),
    ("Rev 21:12", "“the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel”", "The gates carry the tribes’ names"),
    ("Rev 21:14", "“the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb”", "The foundations carry twelve names"),
    ("Rev 21:24", "“the nations of them which are saved shall walk in the light of it”", "The nations are distinct from the city"),
    ("Rev 22:17", "“the Spirit and the bride say, Come”", "The bride speaks with the Spirit"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "8.5 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say that Gentile believers of every age are outside the blessing of the new earth. It says that the nations are named apart from the city and walk in its light.",
    "It does not give a full account of who dwells in the city or of Hebrews 12 and Galatians 4. Those passages are answered in Chapter 13 as objections to the position, and the whole subject of the city belongs to the volume of this series on the eschatological community.",
    "It does not say that the apostles on the foundations are the whole of the bride. It says what the text says: twelve names are set in the foundations and twelve names are set on the gates.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "The angel promises to show the bride and shows the city. The city’s gates carry the twelve tribes of Israel, the language of its people is the language of the covenant, the nations walk in its light, and the bride calls with the Spirit at the close of the book."),
]
